During the review cycle, the reviewers raised several core concerns regarding:

**(1)**  The scope of our rotation modeling (SO(2) vs. full 3D motion);

**(2)**  Whether the proposed Lie-group biases truly address cross-frame misalignment and temporal artifacts;

 **(3)**  The necessity of a controlled and fair comparison against optical-flow-based modulation;

 **(4)**  Figure inconsistencies and the need for clearer interpretability evidence.  

We addressed all of these issues through substantial new experiments, controlled analyses, and revisions to both the main text and the appendix.

For point (1), we clarified the design motivation and limitations of using SO(2) as our base Lie group. We have added explanations showing that (i) SO(2) captures the dominant small-angle in-plane motion present in adverse-weather datasets, (ii) operating in the tangent space enables stable optimization and guarantees valid rotations through the exponential map, and (iii) the formulation is mathematically extensible to SE(3), which we now explicitly discuss as a natural direction for future work. These clarifications are incorporated into both the main text (§4.4) and Appendix A.1 to ensure that the paper’s scope and applicability are correctly understood.

In particular, we implemented the controlled comparison explicitly requested by Reviewer ajhU, constructing an optical-flow-based bias module under strictly identical Transformer backbone, training protocol, and bias-fusion scheme. Under these fully matched conditions, our Lie-rotation module achieves **34.06 dB**, outperforming the optical-flow-derived bias (**31.63 dB**) by **+2.43 dB**, directly validating our core claim that Lie-algebra rotation provides a stronger and more stable inductive bias than flow-based representations. We additionally added new attention-difference visualizations, controlled misalignment-correction examples, and high-resolution qualitative results. All controlled comparisons have been **integrated into the main ablation study**, with extended results included in **Appendix**.

Importantly, after examining these updates, **the previously skeptical reviewer (ajhU)**—who initially assigned a *score of 2*—confirmed that the new controlled experiment directly supports the paper’s central claim and subsequently **raised the score to 6**. Due to the recent OpenReview platform bug, this upward score change is not currently displayed; we hope to clearly communicate here that the reviewer acknowledged the revision and updated their evaluation accordingly.

We believe these comprehensive revisions, strengthened analyses, and the decisive controlled experiment fully address the reviewers’ concerns. Thank you very much for your time and consideration.
